WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- tcpdump captures raw packets passing through a network interface, live
- -c <n> limits capture to exactly n packets, then stops automatically
- -n skips hostname/port name resolution - faster and often clearer for analysis
- -A shows packet payload as readable ASCII text (great for seeing plain-text protocols like HTTP)
- -v/-vv add more header detail per packet
- -w saves raw packets to a .pcap file; -r reads one back
- A .pcap file is a specific binary format, not plain text - meant for tools like tcpdump/Wireshark

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] ip addr  (finding my interface)
[ ] sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -c 10  /  -c 10 -n
[ ] sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -c 5 -A  /  -v
[ ] sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -c 20 -w capture.pcap
[ ] tcpdump -r capture.pcap

paste your real output here :
$ sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -c 10 -n
> 

$ sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -c 5 -A
> 

$ sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -c 20 -w capture.pcap  +  tcpdump -r capture.pcap
> 


